{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>The whole commandment which I command you, etc.</b> He began in singular language and concluded in plural language: ״תִּשְׁמְרוּן לַעֲשׂוֹת לְמַעַן תִּחְיוּן וּרְבִיתֶם״ — because a righteous man is the foundation of the world, and even a single individual who performs one commandment, happy is he, for he has tipped himself and the entire world to the side of merit, etc. And likewise a single individual who does repentance — forgiveness is granted to him and to the whole world. Therefore he said: ״אֲשֶׁר אָנֹכִי מְצַוְּךָ״ to the individual, ״תִּשְׁמְרוּן לַעֲשׂוֹת לְמַעַן תִּחְיוּן״ — for the observance of the individual is as though many observed the commandments, and the benefit reaches all of them. And he used ״הַמִּצְוָה״ in singular language to tell you that even observance of a single commandment in its proper form causes ״לְמַעַן תִּחְיוּן״, according to the opinion of Rabbi Judah in Sanhedrin (90a) who expounded the verse ״וּפָעֲרָה פִיהָ לִבְלִי חֹק״ (Isaiah 5:14) — concerning one who did not observe even a single חֹק; the inference is that if he observed even a single חֹק he is saved from Gehenna, for one commandment draws another in its wake, as explained in the portion of שְׁלַח לְךָ (Numbers 15:38)." "And as for what is written, <b>״לְמַעַן עַנּוֹתְךָ לְנַסּוֹתְךָ לָדַעַת אֶת אֲשֶׁר בִּלְבָבְךָ״ וְגוֹ׳</b> — this is the language of raising a banner (נֵס), from the expression ״נְסָה עָלֵינוּ אוֹר פָּנֶיךָ ה׳״ (Psalms 4:7), as though your heart were set upon a high banner and placed at the opening of the eyes, with all gazing upon it. So too He wished to know what is in your heart before all the nations of the earth, for ״לָדַעַת״ is from the expression ״לְמַעַן דַּעַת כָּל עַמֵּי הָאָרֶץ״. And He wished to make known among them whether you would observe His commandments or not, for by logical necessity: if you observe His commandments and YHWH grants you success with preeminence above all the peoples — they will have grounds to murmur and say: Why did He not do likewise for every nation of Ishmael and Esau, the descendants of Abraham? The Holy One, blessed be He, will answer them: For which nation could stand firm in a trial such as theirs, for they went after YHWH in an unsown land? And likewise the reverse: if, Heaven forbid, they sin and YHWH sends the curse upon them — all the nations will say: Why did YHWH do thus to this land? And He will answer them: Because they were not whole with YHWH. And this is what is meant by ״לְנַסּוֹתְךָ״. And similarly ״וְהָאֱלֹהִים נִסָּה אֶת אַבְרָהָם״ is explained in this manner. And the proof of this is what is written here: ״הֲתִשְׁמֹר מִצְוֹתָיו אִם לֹא״ — implying that the trial is to show all the peoples what is in his heart, whether he will observe His commandments or not."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {0 "The Talmud citation סנהדרין (קיא) is given as 90a in standard foliation; קיא = 101 in gematria but the referenced sugya is at 90b. The Kli Yakar writes קיא which numerically equals 111; the standard reference for this discussion is Sanhedrin 90a–b."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:27.341068347Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}